CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Pasa] 2 (1) The implementation of protection of women and children is carried out based on the principles of:

a. respect for the rights of women and children;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination in the empowerment of women and children;
d. the best interests of women and children; ande. restoration of social and economic rights for women and children who experience acts of violence or neglect. (2) The objectives of implementing protection of women and children include:a. preventing acts of violence or neglect against women and children including trafficking in persons;
b. eliminating all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children:c. protecting and providing a sense of security for women and children;

d. providing services to women and children victims of acts of violence, reporters, and witnesses;

facilitating and mediating domestic disputes to realize harmonious and prosperous household integrity; and seeking compensation/compensation for losses suffered by victims from perpetrators of violence.

Article 4

Every woman and child who becomes a victim of physical or psychological violence, exploitation, or trafficking in persons is entitled to protection and services.

Protection, assistance, and services for victims as referred to in paragraph (1) include:

a. to have their dignity and worth as human beings respected;

b. complaints or requests for protection from the person concerned and/or other parties;

c. confidential handling or placement of the victim in a safe location and house whose whereabouts are kept secret to avoid intimidation and threats;

d. medical services/health care in the form of treatment and recovery of the victim’s injuries or physical condition by medical and paramedical personnel;

e. medicolegal services for evidentiary purposes in the legal field;

f. protection by law enforcement officials;

g. provision of information regarding the rights and legal obligations to the victim or the victim’s guardian, as well as identification of the incident;

h. continuous handling up to the stage of rehabilitation;

i. recovery of physical, psychological, and sexual health in accordance with the suffering experienced by victims of violence;

j. psychological assistance to restore the victim’s traumatic condition and restore the victim’s self-confidence;

k. assistance/companionship for the legal process and the acquisition of the right to restitution or compensation;

l. spiritual guidance services;

m. the right to determine their own decisions;

n. assistance by social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations; and

o. guarantee of rights related to status as a family member and a member of society (social reintegration).

(3) In the event that the victim is a child, in addition to obtaining the rights as referred to in paragraph (2), the child also obtains special rights including:

a. the right to respect and full enjoyment for survival, growth, and development;

b. the right to basic services;

c. the right to equal protection;

d. the right to be free from various stigma;

e. the right to obtain freedom; and

f. the right to obtain compensation from the perpetrator.

CHAPTER V

OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

Article 5

Obligations and responsibilities in providing protection for women and children from acts of violence are the shared responsibility of:

a. the regional government;

b. the community;

c. institutions;

d. the family; and

e. parents.

Article 6

The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 5 letter a include:

a. implementing the obligations for the protection of women and children from acts of violence as determined by the government;

b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children from acts of violence;

c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;

d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;

e. providing Safe Houses for women and children victims of violence;

f. allocating a budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with regional financial capacity; and

g. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;

h. establishing regional technical implementing units for the protection of women and children based on the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 7

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the community as referred to in Article 5 letter b are carried out in the form of community participation.

2. The forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) include:

a. preventing the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children;

b. providing protection to victims;

c. providing emergency assistance;

d. providing information and/or reporting acts of violence against women and children to law enforcement officials or authorized parties, and/or the relevant Agency through UPTD PPA; and

e. participating in the handling of victims of acts of violence.

3. The forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (2) are carried out responsibly in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 11

1. The forms of prevention as referred to in Article 10 are carried out through:

a. socialization activities of laws and regulations related to the protection of children and women to the wider community in the region;

b. activities to disseminate information on the protection of women and children so that it is known by all people in the region through the distribution of brochures, leaflets, pamphlets, or notice boards so that incidents of violence, exploitation, and neglect can be reported as soon as possible and can be handled before reaching the worst condition;

c. training for members who are able to disseminate norms of prohibitions and sanctions to the community;

d. opening public complaint channels by the Regional Government or the Agency through the Technical Service Unit in each area through community networks;

e. strengthening coordination channels with District, Sub-district/Village level Government;

f. immediate handling of community complaints throughout the region and securing subjects of acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking, and neglect; and/or persons; and

g. counseling/socialization in the form of raising awareness of the wider community down to the smallest community groups (families), especially parents, to protect women and children.

(2) The Regional Government through the Agency/UPTD conducts data collection on areas of violence based on the incidence rate and conducts research and assessment of the level of area vulnerability to find solutions to reduce up to stopping acts of violence, exploitation, and neglect in vulnerable point areas.

Article 14

The provision of services and assistance to victims shall be carried out with the principles of: non-discrimination;
a. fast, safe, and empathetic;
b. coordination between government agencies;
c. guarantee of confidentiality;
d. easily accessible; and
e. free of charge.

f.

Article 15

Forms of services for the rights of women and children from acts of violence include:
a. complaint, consultation, and counseling services;
b. assistance services;
c. health services;
d. social rehabilitation services;
e. legal services and/or legal aid; and
f. repatriation and social reintegration services.

Article 16

1. The Regional Government through the Office/UPTD is obliged to carry out economic empowerment for victims of violence, exploitation, and neglect.

(2) Forms of empowerment of victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), include:
a. providing the necessary needs for entrepreneurship training for victims of violence, in order to increase knowledge, attitudes, and business skills;
b. facilitating the implementation of various job training and skills training;
c. providing assistance in developing productive economic businesses;
d. exploring cooperation with small, medium and large companies, as well as financial institutions to develop businesses of victims of violence;
e. seeking the provision of capital for victims of violence; and
f. expanding access to information and promoting the products of victims of violence'

Article 18

(1) The Regional Government establishes partnerships with the business community to support the implementation of protection for women and children in the regional area.

2. Partnership as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out through:a. notification of job opportunity information for women victims of violenceart;
b. education and training for women victims of violence;
c. educational assistance for women and children victims of violence who have been withdrawn from their education; andd. fostering and increasing the economic independence of women victims of violence' (3) Partnership as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2], shall be set forth in the form of a cooperation agreement.

CHAPTER IX

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 19

1. In providing protection for women and children, the community may:a. form family partners at the village/sub-district level by the community;
b. form women and children protection units within community organizations;
c. conduct independent socialization of the rights of women and children;

