CHAPTER XII

INVESTIGATION

Article 23

(1) In addition to general investigating officers, investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children in this Regional Regulation may also be carried out by Civil Servant Investigators whose appointment is in accordance with laws and regulations'.

(2) Civil Servant Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1), are authorized to:
a. receive reports or complaints from someone about the existence of criminal acts of violence against women and children;
b. take first action at that time at the scene and conduct an examination;
c. interrogate a suspect and examine the suspect's identity card;
d. seize goods and/or letters;
e. take fingerprints and photograph a person;
f. summon persons to be heard and examined as suspects or witnesses;
g. bring in experts needed in connection with the examination of the case;
h. stop the investigation after receiving instructions from the General Investigator that there is insufficient evidence, or the incident does not constitute a criminal act and subsequently through the General Investigator notify this matter to the public prosecutor, the suspect or their family; and/or
i. take other actions necessary for the smooth investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children, which according to law can be accounted for.

3. Investigators as referred to in paragraph (2), shall notify the commencement of the investigation and submit the results of their investigation to the public prosecutor through the Investigating Officer of the Police of the Republic of Indonesia, in accordance with the provisions stipulated in the Criminal Procedure Code' v;
.

Article 27

In the case that the perpetrator of a criminal act is a child, the following matters apply:

a. the legal process is carried out humanely by considering that the child still has hope for their future;

b. The investigator must be a person who has competence in special investigation for child handling cases;

c. The investigation is conducted in a closed room and attended only by the advocacy team and the child's parents/guardian;

d. in the investigation process, the child is not placed in general detention, but is entrusted to their family with supervision and guarding;

e. the trial process is carried out specifically in a juvenile court room; and

f. the child's punishment process is placed in a special juvenile correctional institution, with a transition period that may be accompanied by their parents.
